        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Romanticism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 1790s; spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1790–1850: from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> circle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads">Lyrical Ballads</a> of 1798 to the revolutions of 1848; forerunners in the Sturm und Drang of the 1770s, and echoes in music to the end of the 19th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frédéric Chopin</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Changed literature, painting and music for good, made feeling and imagination central to art, and fed nationalism and the study of folklore and history; gave way to Realism after about 1850</span></div>
