    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Romanticism was a movement in literature, art, music and thought that from the 1790s prized feeling, imagination and nature over the rules and reason that had governed the arts of the 18th century.</li><li>Its poets, painters and composers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> and Byron to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Chopin</a>, celebrated the individual, wild landscape, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and folk traditions.</li><li>It gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s nations poets, epics and fairy-tale collections and fed both liberal revolt and conservative nostalgia, until Realism displaced it around the middle of the 19th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The cold, wet summer of 1816, which followed the eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> and his guests indoors at the Villa Diodati by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, where his ghost-story contest led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a> to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

