    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1798</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> and Samuel Taylor Coleridge publish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads">Lyrical Ballads</a>; the Schlegel brothers found the Athenaeum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> publish the first volume of their Children’s and Household Tales</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1816</span><span class="rf-text">A ghost-story contest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> starts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a>, published in 1818</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1822</span><span class="rf-text">Adam Mickiewicz’s Ballads and Romances open the Romantic age of Polish literature</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1824</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> while aiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>’s Hernani causes uproar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; Hector Berlioz’s Symphonie fantastique is first performed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1835</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot</a> publishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a>, the epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, from songs collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">Revolutions sweep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a> and Sándor Petőfi stand among their leaders</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe.jpg" alt="Johann Wolfgang von Goethe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goethe_(Stieler_1828).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph Karl Stieler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word came from “romance”, the medieval tales of chivalry written in the vernacular Romance languages rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. In the 18th century “romantic” described wild scenery or stories like those of the old romances; in the late 1790s the German critic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a> gave it a new sense, as the name of a modern poetry opposed to the rules of classical art. The movement grew both out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and against it. It rejected the faith that universal reason and fixed rules could govern art and life, the mechanical picture of nature, and the cool neoclassical taste of the courts and academies.</p><p>Its roots lay in the 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>’s novel Julie, or the New Heloise of 1761 made feeling and natural scenery central to literature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>’s Philosophical Enquiry of 1757 distinguished the beautiful from the sublime, the thrill of awe before storms, mountains and the infinite. James Macpherson’s Ossian poems, published from 1760 as translations of an ancient Gaelic bard but largely his own work, were admired across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> Walpole’s The Castle of Otranto of 1764 began the Gothic novel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Johann Gottfried Herder</a> taught that every people has its own spirit, expressed in its language and folk songs. In the German Sturm und Drang, “storm and stress”, of the 1770s, young writers rebelled against rules in the name of passion and genius: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-sorrows-of-young-werther">The Sorrows of Young Werther</a> of 1774 moved a generation of readers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a>’s The Robbers of 1781 shook audiences.</p><p>History then gave the movement its energy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> of 1789 thrilled many young writers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> later recalled, “Bliss was it in that dawn to be alive”. The Terror and the long wars that followed brought disillusion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s conquests stirred national resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the German lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, with its factories and swelling cities, made untouched nature and the rural past seem precious; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> wrote of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>’s “dark Satanic Mills”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> and the idealist philosophers who followed him, Johann Gottlieb Fichte and Friedrich Schelling, stressed the creative power of the mind. As aristocratic patronage declined, writers, painters and musicians sold their work to a growing public and came to see themselves as independent individuals.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.jpg" alt="Wanderer above the Sea of Fog" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich,_Caspar_David_-_Wanderer_%C3%BCber_dem_Nebelmeer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caspar David Friedrich, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Romanticism had no single creed, and its followers disagreed about religion and politics, but they shared some convictions. The first was the primacy of feeling and imagination. In his preface to the second edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads">Lyrical Ballads</a> in 1800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> declared that “all good poetry is the spontaneous overflow of powerful feelings” and sought poetry in ordinary life and in the language people really speak, while Samuel Taylor Coleridge gave the supernatural a new imaginative seriousness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a> called Romantic poetry “a progressive, universal poetry”, always developing, never complete, free to mix every genre. Art was to express the inner life of the artist rather than to imitate models, and originality and genius became its highest values.</p><p>Nature was a second conviction. Romantics saw it not as a machine but as a living whole, a source of moral and spiritual feeling and a mirror of the soul, from Wordsworth’s lines on the Wye valley to the solitary figures before mountains and seas in the paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>. They sought the sublime in storms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the ocean, and they gave a new value to childhood, dreams, longing and the irrational. Some found their way back to faith, like François-René de Chateaubriand, whose The Genius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> of 1802 defended the Catholic religion through its beauty, or Friedrich Schlegel, who became a Catholic in 1808; others, like Percy Bysshe Shelley, were rebels against Church and state.</p><p>The third was the past and the people. Romantics loved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, Gothic churches and the legends of chivalry, and they collected folk songs, ballads and fairy tales as the voice of a nation. Achim von Arnim and Clemens Brentano gathered German songs in The Boy’s Magic Horn from 1805, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a> their tales from 1812. This love of the national past could serve liberal hopes of freedom for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Italians and Hungarians, or a conservative longing for a Christian and hierarchical order. E. T. A. Hoffmann called instrumental music “the most romantic of all the arts”, and music, which speaks without words, became for many the highest art of all.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-byron.jpg" alt="Lord Byron" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byron_1813_by_Phillips.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Phillips, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement took shape at the end of the 1790s. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> a circle formed around the brothers August Wilhelm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a>, with Caroline Schlegel, Dorothea Veit, the poet Novalis, Ludwig Tieck and the philosophers Fichte and Schelling, and their journal Athenaeum appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> from 1798 to 1800. In the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> and Coleridge published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyrical-ballads">Lyrical Ballads</a>, which opened with Coleridge’s “The Rime of the Ancyent Marinere” and closed with Wordsworth’s lines written above Tintern Abbey. In 1810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ordered the destruction of all 10,000 printed copies of Germaine de Staël’s On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which introduced the German Romantics to French readers; it appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1813.</p><p>The 1810s and 1820s brought the movement’s fullest flowering. Walter Scott’s Waverley of 1814 set the pattern of the historical novel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>’s Childe Harold’s Pilgrimage, from 1812, made its author the most famous poet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the cold summer of 1816 that followed the eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a>, Byron, Shelley and Mary Godwin, soon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a>, stayed beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, where a ghost-story contest gave rise to her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a>. The second generation of British poets died young: John Keats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1821, Shelley drowned off the Italian coast in 1822 and Byron of fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missolonghi">Missolonghi</a> in 1824, while supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Painters turned to drama and landscape: Friedrich’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog">Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</a> of about 1818, Théodore Géricault’s The Raft of the Medusa of 1819, the skies of J. M. W. Turner and John Constable. Carl Maria von Weber’s opera Der Freischütz triumphed in Berlin in 1821, and in 1822 Adam Mickiewicz’s Ballads and Romances began the Romantic age in Polish literature.</p><p>From the late 1820s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was the movement’s capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>’s preface to his play Cromwell of 1827 attacked the classical rules, and on 25 February 1830 the first night of his Hernani turned into a battle between young Romantics and defenders of the classical stage. That year Eugène Delacroix painted Liberty Leading the People, after the July Revolution, and Hector Berlioz’s Symphonie fantastique was first performed. In 1835 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot</a> published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a>. The revolutions of 1848 brought Romantic poets into politics: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a> directed the foreign policy of the new French republic, and in Pest Sándor Petőfi recited his “National Song” on 15 March. The defeat of most of the revolutions in 1848–1849 is often taken to close the Romantic age.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin.jpg" alt="Frédéric Chopin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frédéric Chopin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederic_Chopin_photo.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis-Auguste Bisson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German Romantics were critics and philosophers as much as poets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a> and his brother August Wilhelm, whose translations of Shakespeare are still read, gave the movement its theory; Novalis, an official of the Saxon salt works who died at twenty-eight in 1801, wrote the Hymns to the Night and an unfinished novel whose “blue flower” became the symbol of Romantic longing; E. T. A. Hoffmann wrote fantastic tales and music criticism. Goethe, revered by most of them, kept his distance, and in 1829 told his assistant Johann Peter Eckermann that he called the classical healthy and the romantic sick.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> Blake, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a> and Coleridge formed an older generation, Byron, Shelley and Keats a younger one. Wordsworth, who lived most of his life in the Lake District, became Poet Laureate in 1843; Byron, aristocratic, scandalous and admired across the continent, gave his name to the brooding, rebellious Byronic hero. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a>, the daughter of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a>, wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> one of the most enduring books of the age. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Chateaubriand, Lamartine, Hugo, Alexandre Dumas and George Sand led in literature, and Delacroix and Berlioz in painting and music. Elsewhere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Adam Mickiewicz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and later in exile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-manzoni">Alessandro Manzoni</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> with his novel The Betrothed, and Ralph Waldo Emerson and the Transcendentalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> gave the movement national forms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-schubert">Franz Schubert</a> set more than six hundred poems to music, and the Polish pianist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frédéric Chopin</a>, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1831 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was crushed, turned the memory of his country into mazurkas and polonaises.</p><p>Romanticism was also a movement of readers and audiences. Young men dressed like Werther and later imitated Byron; volunteers from many countries went to fight for Greek independence; German students gathered at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg-festival">Wartburg Festival</a> of 1817 to call for national unity. Middle-class audiences filled the new public concerts and the recitals of virtuosos such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>, tourists sought out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the English Lakes, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> the salon of Rahel Varnhagen brought writers and thinkers together.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino.jpg" alt="Battle of Navarino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navarino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ambroise Louis Garneray, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Romantics changed the arts for good. They made lyric poetry the voice of personal experience, developed the historical novel and the tale of terror and the fantastic, raised landscape painting to the first rank and made new forms central to music: the art song, the short piano piece, the programme symphony and later the symphonic poem. Their love of the past fostered new branches of scholarship. The Grimms studied the history of the German language as well as its tales, scholars across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> collected ballads and edited medieval epics, and historians such as Jules Michelet wrote vivid narrative histories of their nations. Architects revived the Gothic: work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> Cathedral, left unfinished since the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, resumed in 1842 and was completed in 1880, and after the fire of 1834 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a> was rebuilt in Gothic style by Charles Barry and Augustus Pugin.</p><p>Romanticism also helped to make nations. European sympathy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, roused by Byron and the philhellenes, helped bring the intervention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a> in 1827 and to Greek independence. Under the partitions, Polish poets and musicians kept alive a national culture without a state, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, who founded Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1831, preached the brotherhood of free nations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, Hungarians and others turned their languages into vehicles of literature and national pride.</p><p>Its weaknesses showed too. The cult of genius could excuse self-absorption, and the love of the national past could turn into hostility towards neighbours and minorities. The revolutions of 1848–1849 failed almost everywhere: Petőfi fell in battle in July 1849 and many Romantic exiles never went home. By the 1850s Realism in painting and the novel, represented by Gustave Courbet and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a>, and confidence in positive science had displaced Romantic idealism. In music the Romantic style lived on through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a> to the end of the century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalevala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalevala.jpg" alt="Kalevala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalevala1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elias Lönnrot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much that now seems natural began with the Romantics: the idea of the artist as an original genius expressing an inner self, the value placed on sincerity and emotion, the image of childhood as a time of wonder, summed up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">Wordsworth</a>’s line “The Child is father of the Man”. The Romantic love of wild places changed how Europeans and Americans looked at mountains and moors; in his guide to the Lakes Wordsworth spoke of the district as “a sort of national property”, an idea that later movements for national parks and the protection of landscape took up.</p><p>Its imaginative creations remain everywhere. The Grimms’ tales are read in scores of languages, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> and the Gothic novel are ancestors of science fiction and horror, Scott’s historical novels shaped how the 19th century imagined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the music of Schubert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Chopin</a> and Berlioz fills concert halls. National epics and anthologies compiled in the Romantic age, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a> above all, became foundations of national cultures, and later movements, from Symbolism to the counterculture of the 1960s, have been called heirs of Romanticism.</p><p>Its meaning has long been disputed. In 1924 the American philosopher Arthur O. Lovejoy argued that the word had come to mean so many things that historians should speak of romanticisms in the plural; René Wellek replied in 1949 that the movements shared a common view of imagination, nature, symbol and myth. M. H. Abrams’s The Mirror and the Lamp of 1953 described the change as a turn from art as a mirror held up to nature to art as a lamp lit by the artist’s mind. Isaiah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in lectures given in 1965 and published as The Roots of Romanticism in 1999, called it the greatest single shift in the consciousness of the West. Some critics have blamed Romantic irrationalism and nationalism for later political extremes; others stress its humane and liberal sides and its part in the freeing of nations.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Romanticism was a movement in literature, art, music and thought that from the 1790s to the mid-19th century put feeling, imagination, nature and the individual at the centre of culture. Its poets, painters and composers transformed the arts, revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and folk traditions, and inspired national movements across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, leaving modern ideas of creativity, sincerity and love of wild nature.</em></p>

